  <div class="section-wrapper" id="summary" >
    <div class="section-label"><h2>Summary</h2></div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <div class="cpu_description_vs">The <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s/?_encoding=UTF8&amp;camp=1789&amp;creative=390957&amp;field-keywords=Apple+M4+Pro+%2814-CPU+20-GPU%29&amp;linkCode=ur2&amp;tag=cpmo01-20" class="amazon" rel="nofollow sponsored" target="_blank">Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU)</a> is a powerful processor, tailored for professional applications such as video editing or 3D rendering.<br><br>The <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s/?_encoding=UTF8&amp;camp=1789&amp;creative=390957&amp;field-keywords=Apple+M5&amp;linkCode=ur2&amp;tag=cpmo01-20" class="amazon" rel="nofollow sponsored" target="_blank">Apple M5</a>, on the other hand, is designed as an efficient processor for general use and everyday tasks.<br><br>Both processors are manufactured using the advanced 3 nm fabrication process and are characterized by high energy efficiency. The <strong>Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU)</strong> features a 14-core CPU, a 20-core GPU, 36 MB L2 cache, and 273 GB/s memory bandwidth. The <strong>Apple M5</strong> integrates an optimized CPU, a 10-core GPU, 28 MB L2 cache, and 154 GB/s memory bandwidth.<br><br>In single-core performance tests, both CPUs show comparable values, with the <strong>Apple M5</strong> achieving slightly higher results. For multi-core applications, the Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU) is significantly superior due to its higher core count. For gaming scenarios, the <strong>Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU)</strong> offers better graphics performance and smoother frame rates with its 20-core GPU and higher memory bandwidth.<br><br>Both chips are known for their efficiency. The Apple M5 is designed for maximum performance per watt for everyday use. The <strong>Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU)</strong> delivers higher performance but also remains efficient, though it potentially requires more power and more robust cooling under full load.<br><br>In terms of price, the <strong>Apple M5</strong> offers very good value for money for the general market, while the Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU) is positioned as a premium chip for specialized and demanding applications.
<div class="cpu-lists-wrapper"><div class="cpu-block-wrapper"><h3>Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU)</h3><div class="cpu-block"><ul class="cpu1_list">
<li>High multi-core performance</li>
<li>Superior graphics performance (20-core GPU)</li>
<li>Large memory bandwidth (273 GB/s)</li>
<li>Ideal for professional applications</li>
</ul></div></div><div class="cpu-block-wrapper"><h3>Apple M5</h3><div class="cpu-block"><ul class="cpu2_list">
<li>Very good single-core performance</li>
<li>High energy efficiency for everyday use</li>
<li>Excellent value for money</li>
<li>Balanced for general tasks</li>
</ul></div></div></div> </div>
      <div class="vs-verdict vs-verdict--after-text"><h3>Verdict: Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU) or Apple M5?</h3><p>Which processor leads depends on the workload: Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU) and Apple M5 each win one of the two disciplines. In single-core performance, which matters for games, office work and everyday responsiveness, the Apple M5 is 7% ahead of the Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU). In multi-core workloads such as video editing, exporting and heavy multitasking, the Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU) leads the Apple M5 by 51%. On power draw the Apple M5 is rated lower at 17 W versus 50 W for the Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU); in a notebook that mainly affects battery life and fan noise.</p><h3>The most important differences</h3><ul class="vs-verdict-list"><li><span class="vs-verdict-label">TDP</span><span class="vs-verdict-values"><span class="vs-verdict-value">Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU): <strong>50 W</strong></span><span class="vs-verdict-value">Apple M5: <strong>17 W</strong></span></span></li><li><span class="vs-verdict-label">Technology</span><span class="vs-verdict-values"><span class="vs-verdict-value">Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU): <strong>3 nm</strong></span><span class="vs-verdict-value">Apple M5: <strong>3 nm</strong></span></span></li><li><span class="vs-verdict-label">Release date</span><span class="vs-verdict-values"><span class="vs-verdict-value">Apple M4 Pro (14-CPU 20-GPU): <strong>Q4/2024</strong></span><span class="vs-verdict-value">Apple M5: <strong>Q4/2025</strong></span></span></li></ul></div>
    </div>
  </div>




